;;; `std.closure/infer-closure` — closed and **owing**: a state that can never
;;; meet its requirement, and the word for it.
;;;
;;; `02_closed_and_satisfied.ein` with one fact deleted — the witness edge
;;; `(r a1 b1)`. Until M1d S1d.2.6 **the verdict did not move**: both reported
;;; `Solution`, and that standstill was the finding this file was banked to
;;; hold still. It moves now. This half reports
;;;
;;;     solutions (k)   0
;;;     open states     1
;;;     verdict         Open — owes 1 (r: 1)
;;;
;;; and the twin still reports `Solution`. One fact apart, and now one word
;;; apart.
;;;
;;; ── Why nothing catches it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `a1` is an `A`, `r` is declared total, and `r` has no edges. So the
;;; requirement *every A has some B* is unmet. It is also **unreachable**:
;;;
;;;   - no rule derives an `r`-fact — there is no rule here but the observer;
;;;   - no hypothesis can propose one, because `functional ∧ total` derived
;;;     `(__closed__ r)` and closure is exactly the instruction never to
;;;     speculate about `r`.
;;;
;;; And `std.algebra/total` — the scan whose job is to say *the requirement
;;; has become unreachable* — cannot fire, because it is written
;;; open-world-safe: it demands a **stored negative** for every candidate
;;; (`(forall ?b (?isa ?b ?B) (not (?R ?a ?b)))`) before it concludes
;;; anything, and in a program with no negative-completion rule no such
;;; negative will ever be stored. The scan is activated, correct, and silent.
;;;
;;; Nothing is left to check the requirement, so the engine falls back on the
;;; only completeness test it has — *does the generator propose anything?* —
;;; and the generator, having been closed, proposes nothing. Complete by
;;; exhaustion; owing, by discharge.
;;;
;;; ── The three ingredients, and each is individually reasonable ─────────────
;;;
;;; Worth stating because no one of them is the bug:
;;;
;;;   1. `infer-closure`'s operational witness is opt-in and boxed-warned
;;;      (`01_infer_closure.ein` exhibits its completeness hazard);
;;;   2. `total`'s stored-negative discipline is what stops it firing `(false)`
;;;      on every empty-yet state, and is the *right* way to write it —
;;;      `connex` is what the other way looks like;
;;;   3. `complete(kb)` meaning "the generator proposes nothing" is what the
;;;      engine has, because it has no vocabulary for *what a state owes*.
;;;
;;; The corner is their intersection, and it is the closed-and-owing case
;;; [S1d.2.2](../../../docs/history/m1d_satisfiability/README.md#s1d22--the-domain-contract)
;;; asks who is allowed to say `(false)` about.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, decided by the user 2026-08-25: nobody, still ──────────────
;;;
;;; `(false)` is a **derived** refutation — a rule fired and named a conflict —
;;; and no rule fires here. Promoting on "the debt is unpayable" instead would
;;; be a closed-world inference the engine refuses everywhere else, and it
;;; would erase the one distinction the domain contract called strictly more
;;; informative than the verdict: *owes, and cannot pay* against *owes, and
;;; might*. So the word is `Open`, and the unreachability is said where it can
;;; be said precisely — in the rung report, which for this file is
;;;
;;;     rung  mode=stuck  owed=1  branches=0  declined=1
;;;
;;; `declined=1` because `r` is `(__closed__ r)` and a closed relation is
;;; scoped out of hypothesis generation: the debt is owed and unbranchable,
;;; which is exactly *cannot pay*. A state owing only such debts is **stuck**,
;;; reported, never silently complete.
;;;
;;; ── What changed in the file, and it is one line ───────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(total-owed r is-a)`. Ingredient 3 above was *"`complete(kb)` meaning the
;;; generator proposes nothing is what the engine has, because it has no
;;; vocabulary for what a state owes"* — and since S1d.2.4 it has one. The
;;; vocabulary only reaches a program that **uses** it: S1d.2.6's scope rule
;;; judges by discharge where an obligation was stated and by exhaustion where
;;; none was, so declaring the dual is what moves this file and nothing else
;;; would have.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation observes ──────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The same lift as the twin: `closed-here` makes the derived closure
;;; nameable. The expectation says the closure was derived — it does **not**
;;; say the state is a model, and it still cannot: all three `:expect` forms
;;; are assertions about *facts* and an `open` conclusion is never one. What
;;; S1d.2.6 changed is that the claim below is now checked against an **open
;;; state** rather than a model, which is why it holds unchanged — the facts a
;;; state reached are the facts it reached, whatever the verdict calls it.
;;; Growing `:expect` a word for the verdict is P1d.4's.

(import std.algebra :symbols (functional total total-owed))
(import std.closure :symbols (infer-closure))

(relation is-a        T T :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation r           A B :why "{?1} is assigned {?2}")
(relation closed-here T   :why "{?1}'s extension was closed by infer-closure")

(is-a a1 A)
(is-a b1 B) (is-a b2 B)

;; The markers `infer-closure` reads (arity 1) …
(functional r)
(total      r)
;; … and the activator the unreachability scan needs (arity 2). It is
;; activated here and it never fires — see above.
(total r is-a)
;; … and the obligation dual, which does not fire either — it *reports*. This
;; single line is what puts the program in the read-out's scope and moves the
;; verdict from `Solution` to `Open`.
(total-owed r is-a)

(rule closed-observed ()
  :match  (__closed__ ?R)
  :assert (closed-here ?R)
  :priority 500
  :why    "{?R}'s extension is closed")

;; No `(r a1 b1)`. `a1` owes a `B` it can never be given.

(query
  :goal (closed-here ?R)
  ;; `r` is closed by derivation; the other two are scoped out by hand so the
  ;; blind generator is quiet and the verdict is about the requirement alone.
  :no-hypothesis (is-a closed-here)
  :expect (model (closed-here r)))
